{"text": "This section includes only information that changes how support should be provided. For Maya, the pertinent information is: sensory: sensitive to crowded noisy spaces; medical: none affecting school; behaviour: may push work away to request escape; and safety: needs direct teaching for community travel. These details belong in the profile because they affect access, safety, regulation, communication, or independence. They should be shared only with staff who need the information and handled respectfully.\n\nThe priority challenges are the barriers we have already described: abstract language and multi-step directions; generalizing a skill to a new setting; unexpected changes; and reading and mathematics at Grade 8 complexity. The EA’s role is not to diagnose, redesign the student’s program independently, or provide constant help. The EA works under teacher direction and follows the IEP, health and safety plans, behaviour-support plans, and specialist recommendations.\n\nFor Maya, the most useful EA actions are to teach functional goals in authentic contexts; use least-to-most prompting and fade; offer communication before behaviour escalates; and track independence, not only task completion. In practice, this means preparing access before the activity, watching for the student’s communication, using the least intrusive prompt that works, and fading support when the student can take over.\n\nAcross every activity, four priorities remain constant: dignity, safety, access, and growing independence. We can now use the student’s actual learning goals to decide whether the program is adapted or modified.", "voice": "en-GB-SoniaNeural", "audio": "special-education-C-05.mp3", "words": [{"text": "This", "start": 0.1375, "end": 0.3875, "start_char": 0, "end_char": 4}, {"text": "section", "start": 0.4, "end": 0.8875, "start_char": 5, "end_char": 12}, {"text": "includes", "start": 0.9, "end": 1.475, "start_char": 13, "end_char": 21}, {"text": "only", "start": 1.4875, 